    <question xml:id="similarity-another-matrix">
      <title>Another matrix similar to <m>B</m></title>
      <p>
        Consider the matrices
        <md>
<mrow>A' = \frac 15\begin{pmatrix}
-8 \amp -9 \\
6 \amp 13
\end{pmatrix} \qquad
          B = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad
          C' = \frac 12\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp -3 \\
2 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        Then <m>A' = C'B(C')\inv</m>, as one can verify.  Let <m>v_1'=\frac 12{-1\choose 2}</m> and <m>v_2' = \frac 12{-3\choose 1}</m>, the columns of <m>C'</m>, and let <m>\cB' = \{v_1',v_2'\}</m>.  Then <m>A'</m> does the same thing as <m>B</m>, as in <xref ref="similarity-worked-eg"/>, except <m>A'</m> uses the <m>\cB'</m>-coordinate system.  In other words:
        <ul>
          <li><p>
            <m>B</m> scales the <m>e_1</m>-direction by <m>2</m> and the <m>e_2</m>-direction by <m>-1</m>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            <m>A'</m> scales the <m>v_1'</m>-direction by <m>2</m> and the <m>v_2'</m>-direction by <m>-1</m>.
          </p></li>
        </ul>
        <latex-code>
\def\theo{\includegraphics[width=6cm]{theo6.jpg}}

\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.8, thin border nodes]

  \node[transform shape] at (0, 0) {\theo};
  \draw[seq-violet, opacity=1, vector] (0,0) -- (1,0) node[right] {$e_1$};
  \draw[seq-blue,   opacity=1, vector] (0,0) -- (0,1) node[above] {$e_2$};

  \point at (0, 0);

  \begin{scope}[xshift=9cm, xscale=2, yscale=-1]
    \clip (-1.5, -3) rectangle (1.5, 3);

    \node[transform shape] at (0, 0) {\theo};

    \draw[seq-violet, opacity=1, vector] (0,0) -- (1, 0) node[right] {$Be_1$};
    \draw[seq-blue,   opacity=1, vector] (0,0) -- (0, 1) node[below] {$Be_2$};

    \point at (0, 0);
  \end{scope}

  \begin{scope}[yshift=-9cm]
    \clip (-3, -3) rectangle (3, 3);
